  <!--
      ==================== segmented meters ====================

      Two meters in the app share one construction: the onboarding wizard's progress rail and the PIN
      strength meter. Both show a small whole number out of four, and both are built from four fixed
      segments rather than one proportional bar.

      Why segments. A proportional bar has to express its width as a fraction of its parent, which in
      WPF means either a converter returning a GridLength or a binding to the parent's ActualWidth —
      both of which make the meter depend on layout having already run, and the second of which
      re-enters layout when it changes. Four segments have no such dependency. They are also the honest
      presentation of a quantity that is itself discrete: a PIN scores 0, 1, 2, 3 or 4, and a smoothly
      sliding bar would imply a precision the score does not have.

      Why two Borders per segment. The unlit track has to stay visible underneath, so the lit part is a
      second Border on top rather than a Background swap. That also puts the threshold — which segment
      this is — at the use site as a converter parameter, instead of forcing four near-identical styles
      with the number baked into a DataTrigger. The colour logic stays here, in one place, and the view
      says only which segment it is.

      Why the lit layer uses Visibility and not Opacity. IntAtLeast returns a Visibility when the
      binding target is one, so a lit segment is a single attribute in the view with no chained
      converter. Nothing here fades: the strength meter changes on a keystroke, and a 140 ms fade would
      leave the caret ahead of the meter and, worse, leave the word "Strong" on screen beside a segment
      still at half opacity. The meter and its label must agree at every frame.

      Neither meter is interactive. The rail is not navigation — a wizard step is reached by completing
      the one before it — and the strength meter is a readout. Neither takes focus or hit-testing.
  -->

  <!--
      The unlit track of one wizard progress-rail segment.

      No Width: the segment stretches into its Grid column, so the rail is as wide as whatever it sits
      under and the four segments stay equal at any window size.

      Height 4 with Radius.Small, which is also 4 — WPF clamps a corner radius to half the smaller side,
      so this renders as a fully rounded 4px bar. That is the intended shape and it needs no new token;
      Radius.Switch stays the only radius that exists to make something a pill on purpose.
  -->
  <Style x:Key="Rail.Segment" TargetType="Border">
    <Setter Property="Height" Value="4" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource Radius.Small}" />
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource Surface.Raised}" />
    <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Stretch" />
    <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
    <Setter Property="SnapsToDevicePixels" Value="True" />
  </Style>

  <!--
      The lit part of a rail segment. Sits in the same Grid cell as its track, above it in z-order.

      Accent for every lit segment: the rail says how far through setup the user has come, and the steps
      are not ranked against each other, so there is nothing for a second colour to mean.
  -->
  <Style x:Key="Rail.Segment.Fill" TargetType="Border" BasedOn="{StaticResource Rail.Segment}">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource Accent}" />
  </Style>

  <!--
      The PIN strength meter's unlit track. Its own key rather than a reuse of Rail.Segment, so that a
      later change to the wizard's rail cannot silently restyle a security control.
  -->
  <Style x:Key="Strength.Segment" TargetType="Border" BasedOn="{StaticResource Rail.Segment}" />

  <!--
      The lit part of a strength segment. One style serves all four, because the colour of a lit segment
      depends only on the score as a whole — a score of 2 lights two segments and both of them are Fair
      — so these triggers never need to know which segment they are on.

      Bound to the string StrengthLevel rather than to the numeric StrengthScore, so the bars and the
      word beside them read from one property and cannot disagree.

      The ladder is State.Bad, State.Warn, Accent, State.Good. Accent rather than a second amber for
      Good, because Good is the point at which the PIN is acceptable and the app's own colour says that
      without claiming it is the best available; green is held back for Strong. Colour is never the only
      signal here — Strength.Label spells the level out immediately beside the bars.

      The default is State.Bad: a lit segment whose level matched none of the triggers would mean a
      view-model string the view does not know, and red is the reading that does not overstate a secret.
  -->
  <Style x:Key="Strength.Segment.Fill" TargetType="Border" BasedOn="{StaticResource Rail.Segment}">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource State.Bad}" />
    <Style.Triggers>
      <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding StrengthLevel}" Value="Fair">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource State.Warn}" />
      </DataTrigger>
      <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding StrengthLevel}" Value="Good">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource Accent}" />
      </DataTrigger>
      <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding StrengthLevel}" Value="Strong">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource State.Good}" />
      </DataTrigger>
    </Style.Triggers>
  </Style>

  <!--
      The word beside the strength meter. It repeats the fill's trigger ladder so that the label and the
      bars are coloured from the same property, and it is the accessible statement of strength rather
      than a caption on one: a Border carries no useful automation text, so a screen-reader user reads
      the level here or does not read it at all. The view sets AutomationProperties.LiveSetting="Polite"
      on this element, which announces a change in level without cutting across the user's typing.
  -->
  <Style x:Key="Strength.Label" TargetType="TextBlock" BasedOn="{StaticResource Text.Caption}">
    <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold" />
    <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource State.Bad}" />
    <Style.Triggers>
      <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding StrengthLevel}" Value="Fair">
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource State.Warn}" />
      </DataTrigger>
      <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding StrengthLevel}" Value="Good">
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource Accent}" />
      </DataTrigger>
      <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding StrengthLevel}" Value="Strong">
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource State.Good}" />
      </DataTrigger>
    </Style.Triggers>
  </Style>

  <!--
      Usage, wizard rail. Four star columns, both layers of a segment in the same cell, and an 8px
      gutter on every segment except the first. StepIndex is 1 based, so step 1 lights one segment.

      <Grid VerticalAlignment="Center">
        <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
          <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
          <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
          <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
          <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
        </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

        <Border Style="{StaticResource Rail.Segment}" />
        <Border Style="{StaticResource Rail.Segment.Fill}"
                Visibility="{Binding StepIndex, Converter={StaticResource IntAtLeast},
                                     ConverterParameter=1}" />

        <Border Grid.Column="1" Style="{StaticResource Rail.Segment}"
                Margin="{StaticResource Space.Inline.S}" />
        <Border Grid.Column="1" Style="{StaticResource Rail.Segment.Fill}"
                Margin="{StaticResource Space.Inline.S}"
                Visibility="{Binding StepIndex, Converter={StaticResource IntAtLeast},
                                     ConverterParameter=2}" />

        <Border Grid.Column="2" Style="{StaticResource Rail.Segment}"
                Margin="{StaticResource Space.Inline.S}" />
        <Border Grid.Column="2" Style="{StaticResource Rail.Segment.Fill}"
                Margin="{StaticResource Space.Inline.S}"
                Visibility="{Binding StepIndex, Converter={StaticResource IntAtLeast},
                                     ConverterParameter=3}" />

        <Border Grid.Column="3" Style="{StaticResource Rail.Segment}"
                Margin="{StaticResource Space.Inline.S}" />
        <Border Grid.Column="3" Style="{StaticResource Rail.Segment.Fill}"
                Margin="{StaticResource Space.Inline.S}"
                Visibility="{Binding StepIndex, Converter={StaticResource IntAtLeast},
                                     ConverterParameter=4}" />
      </Grid>

      The gutter is repeated on both layers of a segment because a Style cannot carry it: the first
      segment must not have one, and the alternative is a fifth style differing only in a margin.
  -->

  <!--
      Usage, PIN strength meter. The same four cells, with the label in a fifth Auto column so the bars
      keep the width the text does not need.

      <Grid Margin="{StaticResource Space.Stack.S}">
        <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
          <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
          <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
          <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
          <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
          <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
        </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

        <Border Style="{StaticResource Strength.Segment}" />
        <Border Style="{StaticResource Strength.Segment.Fill}"
                Visibility="{Binding StrengthScore, Converter={StaticResource IntAtLeast},
                                     ConverterParameter=1}" />

        (columns 1, 2 and 3 repeat with Margin="{StaticResource Space.Inline.S}"
         and ConverterParameter 2, 3 and 4)

        <TextBlock Grid.Column="4" Style="{StaticResource Strength.Label}"
                   Margin="{StaticResource Space.Inline.M}"
                   Text="{Binding StrengthLevel}"
                   AutomationProperties.LiveSetting="Polite" />
      </Grid>

      StrengthScore and StrengthLevel are pushed onto the view model from the PasswordBox's
      PasswordChanged handler in code behind, because PasswordBox.Password is not a dependency property
      and cannot be bound. That is already how the onboarding and Settings secret boxes reach their view
      models, so the meter adds no new mechanism — only two more properties for the existing handler to
      set.
  -->




</ResourceDictionary>
